%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 98395f251812ec84d8bbc703ba333038, type: 3}
  m_Name: Baguio_Quiz
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  quizId: Q1
  stageId: 1
  topics:
  - id: warming
    name: Cause of warming
  - id: sinks
    name: Forests & carbon
  - id: target
    name: "The 1.5 \xB0C target"
  - id: wildlife
    name: Forests & wildlife
  - id: geothermal
    name: Clean energy
  - id: risk
    name: Risk to the Philippines
  - id: game
    name: Game check
  preQuestions:
  - questionId: S1_PRE_1
    topicId: warming
    text: Global temperatures have risen over the past 150 years. What is the main
      cause?
    choices:
    - Natural changes in the amount of energy from the Sun
    - The hole in the ozone layer letting in more of the Sun's heat
    - Greenhouse gases released by burning fossil fuels and clearing forests
    - Heat and ash released by volcanic eruptions
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_PRE_2
    topicId: sinks
    text: The pine forests around Baguio are called "carbon sinks." What does that
      mean?
    choices:
    - They take in CO2 and store the carbon in their wood, roots and soil
    - They release more oxygen than any other place, and oxygen cools the planet
    - Their soil soaks up rainwater, which pulls heat out of the air
    - They turn all the carbon they take in into oxygen, with no limit
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_PRE_3
    topicId: target
    text: "What does the 1.5 \xB0C target in the Paris Agreement refer to?"
    choices:
    - "Lowering today's global temperature by 1.5 \xB0C"
    - "Limiting global warming to 1.5 \xB0C above pre-industrial levels"
    - "Keeping each country's warming below 1.5 \xB0C per year"
    - The amount of warming at which sea levels start to rise
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_PRE_4
    topicId: wildlife
    text: How does protecting a forest like the Sierra Madre help both wildlife and
      the climate?
    choices:
    - Forest animals breathe in CO2, so more animals means less CO2
    - It helps wildlife, but cutting the trees and replanting elsewhere is just as
      good for the climate
    - It helps wildlife only; the climate depends on factories and cars
    - It keeps the animals' habitat and keeps the forest's carbon stored
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_PRE_5
    topicId: geothermal
    text: The Philippines is one of the world's largest producers of geothermal energy.
      What is geothermal energy?
    choices:
    - Electricity made by burning natural gas found underground
    - Electricity made from the Earth's natural heat, releasing very little CO2
    - Electricity from sunlight stored as heat in rocks
    - Electricity from hot water released by coal plants
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_PRE_6
    topicId: risk
    text: "Why is keeping warming below 1.5 \xB0C especially important for the Philippines?"
    choices:
    - The country faces stronger typhoons, floods, rising seas and heat waves as
      the planet warms
    - Warming would make the Philippines too cold for rice farming
    - The Philippines releases most of the world's greenhouse gases
    - Climate change mainly affects countries near the North and South Poles
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  postQuestions:
  - questionId: S1_POST_1
    topicId: warming
    text: Which of these activities adds the most to the gases that trap heat in
      the atmosphere?
    choices:
    - Burning coal, oil and gas for electricity and transport
    - Using aerosol sprays that damage the ozone layer
    - Steam coming out of power plant cooling towers
    - Forest fires started naturally by lightning
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_POST_2
    topicId: sinks
    text: Loggers clear a hillside forest and burn the wood. What happens to the
      carbon the trees had stored?
    choices:
    - It stays locked in the soil, so the climate is not affected
    - It turns into oxygen when the wood burns
    - It is destroyed by the fire and disappears
    - It goes back into the air as CO2 and adds to warming
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_POST_3
    topicId: target
    text: "A news report says, \"The world is getting close to passing 1.5 \xB0C.\"
      What is being measured?"
    choices:
    - How much hotter this year's summer is than last year's
    - The average temperature of the Pacific Ocean
    - How much warmer the Earth's average surface is than before industrialization
    - The highest temperature recorded in the Philippines this year
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_POST_4
    topicId: wildlife
    text: The Philippine Eagle lives in large, old forests. If its forest is cleared,
      what is most likely?
    choices:
    - The eagle moves to a city park and the climate is unaffected
    - The eagle loses its home and the stored carbon is released, so wildlife and
      climate both suffer
    - Only the eagle is affected, because the trees grow back within a year
    - The climate improves because fewer trees are using up oxygen
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_POST_5
    topicId: geothermal
    text: Some Philippine provinces get their power from geothermal plants. Why is
      this better for the climate than a coal plant?
    choices:
    - It burns underground fuel more cleanly than coal
    - It makes more electricity per day than any other source
    - Its power plants absorb CO2 from the air
    - It uses the Earth's heat instead of burning fuel, so it releases very little
      CO2
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_POST_6
    topicId: risk
    text: Which of these is a climate change impact that Filipino communities face
      greater risk from?
    choices:
    - More earthquakes along fault lines
    - Longer and stronger volcanic eruptions
    - Stronger typhoons and coastal flooding from rising seas
    - Colder nights during the rainy season
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S1_GAME_1
    topicId: game
    text: In Thermo-Tactics, what do the enemies coming down the road stand for?
    choices:
    - Wild animals that damage farms
    - Fossil fuel emissions and environmental damage, such as illegal logging, mining
      and smog
    - Typhoons that hit Baguio every year
    - Tourists who crowd the city
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 0
  - questionId: S1_GAME_2
    topicId: game
    text: What does deploying a unit to defend a lane represent?
    choices:
    - Protecting nature and cutting emissions before they heat the planet
    - Building more roads to move traffic faster
    - Planting trees only after the damage is done
    - Moving people away from the mountains
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 0
